<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casablanca</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Casablanca is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and its chief port, on the Atlantic coast. Rebuilt in the 18th century on the site of the ruined town of Anfa, it was bombarded and occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1907, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> Resident-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey">Hubert Lyautey</a> developed its modern port and planned European city. It was the scene of the Casablanca Conference of January 1943 between Roosevelt and Churchill.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casablanca.jpg" alt="Casablanca" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chah.salah · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_finance_city_6_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Casablanca stands on the Atlantic coast of the Chaouia plain in the central-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. It has about 3.22 million inhabitants in the urban area, making it the most populous city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, and its port is one of the largest artificial ports in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Before the 15th century the settlement was called Anfa. The sociologist André Adam favoured a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Tamazight</a> origin for the name, such as anfa meaning hill or promontory on the sea, though he found the information insufficient to say which.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> settled the area by the seventh century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used it as a port. Under the Merinids in the 14th century Anfa rose in importance as a port, and after the last Merinids were ousted in 1465 it became independent again, a safe harbour for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and privateers. The Portuguese bombarded it into ruins in 1468, and the town that grew up around it was called Casa Branca, white house.</p><p>Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-iii-of-morocco">Mohammed ben Abdallah</a> built the port and rebuilt the town between 1756 and 1790, after the earthquake of 1755. It was named ad-Dār al-Bayḍāʼ, the Arabic translation of Casa Branca. In the 19th century it grew as a supplier of wool to the British textile industry. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> of 1906 formalised French preeminence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> of March 1912 established the French protectorate. The population rose from about 12,000 to 110,000 by 1921, largely through shanty towns.</p><p>Casablanca hosted the Anfa, or Casablanca, Conference in January 1943, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> discussed the progress of the war, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> attending in minor roles. In the 1940s and 1950s it was a major centre of anti-French rioting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casablanca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tourism, 7 percent of GDP, drew a record 14.5 million visitors in 2023 and nearly 16 million in 2024; Tanger-Med is the largest port in Africa and the Mediterranean, handling over 9 million containers, and Africa's first high-speed railway opened between Tangier and <b>Casablanca</b> in 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>As seventeen African states won independence in 1960, they divided between the <b>Casablanca</b> Group, which backed Nkrumah’s call for rapid union, and the larger Monrovia Group, which preferred cooperation among sovereign states.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In Lewis Milestone's film All Quiet on the Western Front (1930) it accompanies schoolboys leaving their classroom to enlist; in Jean Renoir's La Grande Illusion (1937) and in <b>Casablanca</b> (1942) it is sung by Germans and drowned out by &quot;La Marseillaise&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Casablanca</b> Conference agreed to invade Sicily and intensify the bombing of Germany.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Casablanca</b>, starring Humphrey Bogart and Ingrid Bergman, premiered at the Hollywood Theatre weeks after the Allies landed in North Africa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Lyautey built new European cities beside the old medinas and developed <b>Casablanca's</b> port.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
